//
// PROBE: `!` applied to a CLASS.
// Pins: reference/rust_multistackvm/src/stdlib/execute.rs:86-88 — the CLASS
//       arm delegates to stdlib::bund_execute::execute_class.
// Why:  the corpus executes OBJECTs (`:Value object !`,
//       reference/Bund/examples/object_oriented_programming/value_demo.bund:19)
//       but never a class value directly. Object and class are separate arms.
//
// STATUS: fails today. Recorded as F16, dispositioned FIX; the intended
// behaviour is D23 (`<class> !` creates an object of that class, registered or
// not) with D25 (an anonymous class takes its `.class_name` from itself).
// This probe is the proof of that fix — when Bund2 implements it, the probe
// must stop erroring.
//
// Two wrong turns on the way here, both worth keeping:
//
//   1. `:Probe class ... register` then `:Probe !` does not reach the CLASS
//      arm at all. `:Probe` is an atom, so it pushes a STRING and `!` takes
//      the PTR|STRING|CALL arm (execute.rs:26-33), trying to call a *word*
//      named "Probe". `register` files a class in the class registry, not the
//      word table, so the oracle answered:
//        "i(Probe) for stack returned: Inline Probe not registered"
//
//   2. Executing the class value itself does reach the CLASS arm, and the arm
//      is broken. `execute_class` pushes the CLASS value and calls
//      `stdlib_object_inline` (bund_execute/execute_class.rs:8-11), which
//      immediately cast_string()s its operand to get a class *name*
//      (bund_object.rs:179). A CLASS is not a string, so the oracle answered:
//        "OBJECT returns error: This Dynamic type is not string"
//
// The class below names itself, per D25 — the same thing every built-in class
// does (base_classes.rs:96). That is what supplies `.class_name` for an
// object built from a class that was never registered.
//

:Probe class
  :.class_name "Probe" set
  :greet { "probe: class arm reached the member" println } set
!
